 ## Abstract
 
-Long-horizon document intelligence systems are usually scored on final-answer correctness, which cannot distinguish a defect observed by an intermediate checkpoint from one that was silently absorbed, nor reveal that the evaluator itself was blind to the defect. We study evaluation-protocol validity rather than proposing a stronger detector. Our meta-evaluation separates checkpoint-level visibility, protocol-level detection, and equivalence-dependent harmfulness, using hierarchical checkpoint evidence as an instrument and per-family visibility profiles as the reporting surface. On a frozen public tabular corpus containing 95 tables and 118 synthetic header-drift injections, checkpoint-level analysis yields 96/118 visible injections, while OCR-like perturbations are visible in 0/13 cases across five thresholds. End-to-end, flat, and hierarchical protocols detect 112/118, 85/118, and 13/118 injections under different decision signals; after matched-false-positive-rate calibration, flat and hierarchical scores are affinely equivalent with a [0,0] protocol-difference interval because only one scored leaf responds to the injected input. This diagnosis shows that protocol comparisons without calibrated operating points can create misleading superiority stories: hierarchy decomposes evidence but does not improve detection sensitivity or establish localization in the current setting. Our results motivate evaluation reports that publish per-family visibility profiles, explicit construct definitions, validity boundaries, and reproducible evidence chains alongside aggregate scores.
